# Proposed by the swarm (2026-10-14), from the product owner's decision in collab/QUESTIONS.md (what a start may move); the documenter may edit or drop it.
# Story: an editor and a browser are docked, the notes were never docked and float on the Desktop. Framed on the editor, the person
# quits Arbor in the evening: every window comes back into view. In the morning they start it again. No surprises at start: what was docked
# is docked, EVERYTHING shows, and the notes are where they were. The view of last night is one step back; the saved view and the name are kept.
# Reference: T60 T61, issue #3 rule 2.
open Editor 900x700
open Browser 1000x700
open Notes 400x300
dock 101 left
dock 102 right
zoom 101
save-view 1
name writing
expect camera 101
expect hidden 102
expect hidden 103
relaunch
expect ok
expect camera root
expect docked 101
expect docked 102
expect left-of 101 102
expect visible 101
expect visible 102
expect floating 103
expect visible 103
expect size 103 400x300
# Nothing is lost: last night's view is where Back leads, and ⌃⌥1 and the name still know it.
back
expect camera 101
expect hidden 102
show-all
view 1
expect camera 101
expect space writing
# Quit while everything shows, and the next start changes nothing a person can see.
show-all
relaunch
expect camera root
expect visible 101
expect visible 102
expect visible 103
# A terminal that was summoned and sent away: quitting fetches it like every other window, and the next start leaves it there.
open Terminal 600x400
zoom 101
summon Terminal
focus 101
expect hidden 104
relaunch
expect camera root
expect visible 104
expect size 104 600x400
# A pause is no quit: its end brings the view back.
zoom 102
pause
resume
expect camera 102
expect hidden 101
# But a quit during a pause is a quit, and the next start a start like any other.
pause
relaunch
expect camera root
expect visible 101
expect visible 102
expect visible 103
expect visible 104
